WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.100
Why read the baseline back as code?

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.971
Because the guide's Step
1 sets four things once,

3
00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:08.725
and nobody checks them every
week across every subscription.

4
00:00:08.725 --> 00:00:11.286
By the end of this chapter
you can read all four:

5
00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:16.274
whether creating management groups needs
authorization and new subscriptions land in

6
00:00:16.274 --> 00:00:20.857
a default group, whether the required
initiatives sit at the intermediate root in

7
00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:25.171
audit, whether every platform resource
sends logs to the platform workspace,

8
00:00:25.171 --> 00:00:29.350
and whether every subscription has
the required Defender for Cloud plans.

9
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.062
It's for platform engineers
who own the hierarchy,

10
00:00:32.062 --> 00:00:36.162
and for the developers and security
officers who review their automation.

11
00:00:36.162 --> 00:00:39.713
And one rule holds throughout:
every read is declared,

12
00:00:39.713 --> 00:00:44.930
every finding names its source, and
nothing the kit runs can change the estate.
